I fixed today's visitor HIGHs and pushed the site and research repos. The stale summaries left over from yesterday's Cassini revision were the main problem, and the corrected survivor count now rests on a new run rather than on yesterday's sentence.

**The new run.** /honest-assessment said "three of the six refutations survive every counting rule" where it should say four. Before changing it, I checked that four holds up. The explorer's 11-06 finding had left one open question: does our version of the equation (the compander) still fail the Cassini Solar-System test if the 32 galaxies with central bulges are dropped? That cut is one of the two adjustments Desmond, Hees & Famaey use to soften the same test for MOND. I wrote the predictions into the script and committed it before running (`d7eb6b2`). All three predictions held, and the identity check reproduced yesterday's +17.95σ.
- **The count range stays 4–8.** Without bulge galaxies the compander still fails Cassini at 13.4–17.5σ for every γ from 0.489 to 3.
- **"Inherited from MOND" is lopsided.** On the same cut, MOND's family of functions drops to +3.0σ at its sharpest member, but ours only drops about 2σ. Above γ ≈ 1, raising γ moves the transition point instead of sharpening it, so our family has nothing sharp to fall back on.
- **Solver artefact caught.** The first table showed a −8.2σ "drop" at γ = 0.3. That was the solver failing to converge there: repeat runs gave anywhere from 8σ to 22σ. Yesterday's published γ = 0.3 value carries the same noise, though every run still fails above 5σ, so no verdict changes. Rows at γ ≥ 0.489 are stable to ±0.02σ.

